  "text": "Solution 2 has the correct final answer, 37/30, though its justification is only a bare citation.\n\n**Solution 1 (6086/3465) is wrong.** Its values 2/(g+2) for odd girth g are not the right thresholds, and it never establishes the claimed upper bounds. Its set has infinitely many potential values but only finitely many are listed, so the classification is unsupported.\n\n**Solution 3 (∞) is wrong.** It asserts δ_χ(C_{2k+1},2) = 2/(2k+3) for all k and an unbounded sum. That contradicts the known result that the set of values is small and finite.\n\n**Solution 2 (37/30) matches the known result.** I recall Illingworth's \"Minimum degree stability of H-free graphs\" showing that for every 3-chromatic H, δ_χ(H,2) lies in {1/3, 2/5, 1/2}, and I believe each value is attained. The 2/5 value is consistent with Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós for K_3, which gives δ_χ(K_3,2)=2/5. I did not check the paper directly in this session, so the 1/3 and 1/2 values rest on memory. The sum is 1/3 + 2/5 + 1/2 = 10/30 + 12/30 + 15/30 = 37/30.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
